# {{.project_name}}

Reproducible MLflow experiment project generated from the AAI platform
template (`experiment-starter`). No LLMs — this is the foundation rung of the
template ladder: governed tags, dataset lineage, deterministic metrics,
artifacts, and a release gate.

## Local setup

Authenticate without storing credentials:

```bash
az login
export DATABRICKS_HOST={{.workspace_host}}
export DATABRICKS_AUTH_TYPE=azure-cli
python scripts/install_core.py
python -m pip install -r requirements.lock
python -m pip install -e '.[dev]'
pytest -q
```

## Lifecycle

1. Replace `data/sample.csv` and `score_row` in `src/app/experiment.py` with
   your dataset and model. Keep the recording calls — dataset digest, seed,
   source commit, and environment freeze are what make a rerun possible.
2. Run locally: `python jobs/run_experiment.py` (writes a tracked run to
   `{{.experiment_name}}`).
3. Gate a release: `python evals/evaluate.py` applies the thresholds in
   `evals/gate_config.json`; after a released run passes, refresh the
   regression baseline with `--update-baseline`.
4. Deploy the scheduled job: `databricks bundle validate -t dev` then
   `databricks bundle deploy -t dev`.

Pull-request CI runs only the deterministic checks
(`evals/offline_checks.py`) — it never holds credentials.

The platform team must onboard this repository (main-branch federated
credential, Databricks service principal, constrained compute permission,
non-secret repository variables) before the deploy workflow is enabled.
